{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/trinity/trinity-continuum-aberrant/490262-define-india-syndrome","id":490262,"title":"Define 'India Syndrome'","path":["Home","Onyx Path","Onyx Path Worlds","The Trinity Continuum","Trinity Continuum: Aberrant"],"posts":[{"date":"2015-04-23T23:44:23-07:00","id":490262,"title":"Define 'India Syndrome'","content":"A lot of good posts on here over the last couple of days, i'd love to respond to all of them in the depth i'd prefer - but since i'm a little strapped for time i thought i'd ask a general question that i've found my self considering after some recent discussion.<br /> <br /> What is India Syndrome?<br /> <br /> A quick look-up in the Aberrant Glossary discovered nothing of consequence, but it could be officially defined in one of the other books i'm not looking at. If you can point me to the 'textbook' definition, i'd be much obliged. But i'm more interested in how you personally read this unique, potentially rascist (creedist?), element of the setting. I'm invested because i've actually created and played a character with what i thought was India Syndrome, but some of the posters here tend to vary with my interpretation.<br /> Is this a sociatal effect or a personal one? Does it affect a character's Anima or is it all psychological? If there were pre-hammersmith Novas in your world, how did this figure into that? Was Archangel Michael a sufferer/victim?<br /> Thanks for your attention.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4458-nihilist","name":"Nihilist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4458_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"259"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-24T02:38:58-07:00","id":490357,"title":"","content":"I recall it refers to Novas who believed themselves to be akin to gods and demanded to be worshipped as such.<br /> <br /> I'll dig around in the books and see if I can find where it's discussed (if at all).","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>Writer. Developer.</i> <b>World of Darkness</b> | <b>Chronicles of Darkness</b> | <b>The Trinity Continuum</b>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/156-bunyip","name":"Bunyip","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar156_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1880"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-24T11:25:56-07:00","id":490745,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Bunyip</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/trinity/trinity-continuum-aberrant/490262-define-india-syndrome?p=490357#post490357\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I recall it refers to Novas who believed themselves to be akin to gods and demanded to be worshipped as such.<br /> <br /> I'll dig around in the books and see if I can find where it's discussed (if at all).</div> </div> </div> </div>I just saw this a couple days ago in one of the books, but I can't recall which one. I want to say either the core book or Stellar Frontiers, but I could be wrong.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://imagizer.imageshack.us/v2/125x125q90/835/7xu8.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://imagizer.imageshack.us/v2/125x125q90/546/wpur.jpg\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><br /> <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/folderview?id=0By9UuUWWH6CiMVlhWHMwNWcyaG8&amp;usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">Complete Garou Gifts List, Fera Gifts List, Garou Totems List and other assorted OPP/White Wolf goodies</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2710-su-tehp","name":"Su-tehp","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2710_3.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"532"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-24T12:06:57-07:00","id":490789,"title":"","content":"I don't have my books handy, but I believe India Syndrome came up most prominently in the corebook, and in the splat on mercenaries (particularly the &quot;Tank&quot; npc description). I imagine it also got mentioned in the &quot;Storyteller's screen&quot;, which had a lengthy section on the impact of novas on religion<br /> <br /> As I read it, &quot;India Syndrome&quot; isn't an illness or derangement (<i>a la</i> megalomania), rather its a sociological phenomena. One that has two components. First, its just a (somewhat snide) way to describe novas (and baselines) who see nova abilities from a religious perspective. The view that their powers mark them as a prophet, or the chosen of God, or even a god themselves. And secondly, the tendency of &quot;cults&quot; and quasi-religious organizations to form around such novas.<br /> <br /> And its not entirely unreasonable. In <i>Aberrant</i>, most novas see their abilities through a comic book lens - that their gifts are cool powers brought about by the radiation released by the Galatea explosion. Which, if you think about it, is kind of an odd leap to make. Why wouldn't a religious nova see their powers as a sign from God, particularly if those powers are in line with the miracles traditionally associated with that religion? Wouldn't a pious Christian nova with the power to heal, walk on water, and call down fire from heaven, assume that their new abilities come directly from God? Or even make the leap and assume they're the Second Coming)? And why wouldn't the people in their community agree?<br /> <br /> As for the term itself, it was first mentioned in regards to nova cults in rural India. A lack of education and communication with the outside world resulted in novas who had never heard of N-Day or eruption, who had no idea that there were others like them, and only a religious explanation to go on for their powers. With no alternate origin options presented, these novas believed they were actually the latest avatar of Shiva, or Kali , or (insert traditional Hindu deity's name). . . and behaved accordingly. They appointed priests, made religious decrees, accepted offerings . . .<br /> <br /> And the locals, who came from the same background, supported the whole endeavor. After all, who wants to argue with the living manifestation of a god? Even if they weren't particularly pious before . . . .Its easy to have faith when your god is <i>right there</i>, ten feet away, blessing people and performing miracles. Heck, some India Syndrome cults might have been the one to convince the nova of their &quot;divinity&quot; (and not the other way round).<br /> <br /> Needless to say, &quot;India Syndrome&quot; probably freaked a lot of secularists out.<br /> <br /> But rural India was just an extreme example - it happened elsewhere. Tank, a DeVries operative, was originally an &quot;India Syndrome&quot; nova from Mongolia, for example. Wycliffe, the nova who irradiates the American heartland during the Aberrant War, was born and bred in the USA. And Kamisama Buddhism in Japan is a large scale example of the same phenomena - just with a more sophisticated worldview (since they know there are thousands of novas in the world). I'd even argue that the Teragen (heck, there's even a Cult of Mal) and the inner circle of the Aeon Society are showing signs of India Syndrome, <i>de facto</i> deifying Divis Mal and Maxwell Mercer.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4624-one-vorlon","name":"One Vorlon","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4624_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"537"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-24T15:10:32-07:00","id":490987,"title":"","content":"The Teragen are definitely supposed to be quite cultish, I think. They follow the teachings of a being they consider divine in their own way, despite that being's protestations otherwise.<br /> <br /> I mean, his name is Divis Mal. A Bad God, or Terrible Angel (in slightly mangled Latin). There's definite a theme there.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/117-solar","name":"Solar","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar117_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1267"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-25T11:34:02-07:00","id":491805,"title":"","content":"The impression i'm getting is that 'India Syndrome is interpreted to mean a sociological response by humans toward novas and/or a paradigm of self-divinity by the novas themselves. Perhaps the two are being conflated, because Nova cults are mentioned often with no connection to the term, while the term itself is applied mostly to Novas as opposed to baselines. As per 'Tank' the Mongolian Nova pointed out by One Vorlon, found in the Elites book.<br /> In my brief investigations since i first posted the question i've found no more definition than i had at the beginning - it doesn't come up in the Storyteller screen material on religions, Church of Michael Archangel doesn't bring it up as far as the skim i took was concerned. I'm starting to wonder why i thought i had such a clear idea of it being a kind of common anima 'theme' amongst hindu novas - inevitable because of the extended multi-faceted polytheism involved combined with some of the elements One Vorlon mentioned about religious populations.<br /> But, until i read a definition, or at least something that might dissuade me - i have to stick to my interpretation - if only to balance out the others.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4458-nihilist","name":"Nihilist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4458_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"259"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-26T05:04:07-07:00","id":492529,"title":"","content":"Yeah, 'India Syndrome' refers to humans defaulting to worshipping novas as gods when confronted with quantum powers. It's so called because with India's population density, they've got a lot of novas compared to other countries -- and a sizable number of them erupted in places out in the middle of nowhere where the locals might not be fully aware of novas as a phenomenon and thus have little reason to assume that novas are anything but gods.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Christopher Shaffer, freelancer. Game line contributions: W20, Night Horrors, TC: \u00c6on, Scion (details <a href=\"http://mythicfoxwrites.blogspot.com/2019/07/stuff-ive-worked-on.html\" target=\"_blank\">here</a>) <br /> <a href=\"http://mythicfoxwrites.blogspot.com/2020/10/actual-play-index.html\" target=\"_blank\"> <b>Blog post write-ups of my gaming sessions; Currently running Exalted, and also updating old Promethean sessions</b></a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/171-mythicfox","name":"MythicFox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar171_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"151"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-28T04:11:28-07:00","id":494578,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>MythicFox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/trinity/trinity-continuum-aberrant/490262-define-india-syndrome?p=492529#post492529\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Yeah, 'India Syndrome' refers to humans defaulting to worshipping novas as gods when confronted with quantum powers. It's so called because with India's population density, they've got a lot of novas compared to other countries -- and a sizable number of them erupted in places out in the middle of nowhere where the locals might not be fully aware of novas as a phenomenon and thus have little reason to assume that novas are anything but gods.</div> </div> </div> </div>Purely statistic prediction might have seen the majority erupt in the denser urban areas - but India and China both <i>would</i> have seen disproportionately high eruptions by population. As much as i understand this, the actual number of eruptions in these circumstances would have been low due to the corresponding population density required for this kind of isolation. Making 'India Syndrome' little more than a handful of anomalies by 2008, maybe just blown up by media coverage or something.<br /> I don't know, i still haven't read anything official to disqualify my own version (India Syndrome as a peculiar personal anima &amp; the social response to it as a result but not the cause), so i'm inclined to stay loyal. But i don't want to be misinterpreting the games original vision either, or at least not doing so ignorantly.<br /> Since the official definition isn't yet located, i may have to bite the bullet and go looking myself. If anyone can let me know books they've read recently that defenitely didn't define India Syndrome, post them.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":4458,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4458-nihilist","user":"Nihilist","date":".  demographics... 04-28-2015, 12:07 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4458-nihilist","name":"Nihilist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4458_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"259"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-28T16:56:42-07:00","id":495415,"title":"","content":"Aberrant Player's Guide, page twenty-three:<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> India, with its caste-driven culture, treats novas like virtual gods. In fact, so many cults have sprung up around Indian novas that the term &quot;India Syndrome&quot; has come into popular use, referring to a nova who accepts worship as a god or cultic figure. These Indian &quot;avatars&quot; do as they please in the rural areas of the country. Fortunately, most of these novas are content to use their abilities to protect and help their people. The United Nations and Project Utopia once tried to investigate rumors of dark and bloody nova cults to ensure the safety of India's population, but when India objected, that course of action was terminated. This isn't as problematic as it may sound, however, as those programs were costly and little to no hard evidence of such cults was ever brought to light. </div> </div> </div>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/173-morimaruvik","name":"Morimaruvik","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar173_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"126"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-28T22:38:42-07:00","id":495934,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Morimaruvik</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/trinity/trinity-continuum-aberrant/490262-define-india-syndrome?p=495415#post495415\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Aberrant Player's Guide, page twenty-three:<br /></div> </div> </div> </div>You boss, thanks Morimaruvik.<br /> <br /> You're right, this is as close as i've seen to a definition - India Syndrome is applied correctly to the Nova who 'accepts worship' as a diety - it appears to be a type of psychological diagnosis. The baseline worshippers themselves are still 'Nova cultists' regardless of how their target 'diety' reacts to their faith. I was wrong, good to know.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4458-nihilist","name":"Nihilist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4458_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"259"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-29T01:29:45-07:00","id":496054,"title":"","content":"Thanks Morimaruvik, I'd not managed to locate the reference.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>Writer. Developer.</i> <b>World of Darkness</b> | <b>Chronicles of Darkness</b> | <b>The Trinity Continuum</b>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/156-bunyip","name":"Bunyip","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar156_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1880"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-29T01:46:04-07:00","id":496093,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Bunyip</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/trinity/trinity-continuum-aberrant/490262-define-india-syndrome?p=496054#post496054\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Thanks Morimaruvik, I'd not managed to locate the reference.</div> </div> </div> </div>You were looking all that time? That was one elusive bit of information...<br /> -smile-","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4458-nihilist","name":"Nihilist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4458_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"259"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-29T02:05:09-07:00","id":496099,"title":"","content":"I may have taken breaks from time to time... <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>Writer. Developer.</i> <b>World of Darkness</b> | <b>Chronicles of Darkness</b> | <b>The Trinity Continuum</b>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/156-bunyip","name":"Bunyip","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar156_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1880"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-29T03:48:47-07:00","id":496170,"title":"","content":"How... old is that book?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-29T04:39:25-07:00","id":496181,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isator Levi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/trinity/trinity-continuum-aberrant/490262-define-india-syndrome?p=496170#post496170\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">How... old is that book?</div> </div> </div> </div>Published January 2001.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/51-marin","name":"marin","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2019"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-29T08:54:27-07:00","id":496341,"title":"","content":"I'll admit that my brief looking into it suggests that caste might remain a more significant cultural factor in India than I had previously assumed, but... this still looks like the kind of thing that could do with a bit of updating.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-29T15:03:22-07:00","id":496805,"title":"","content":"Caste is still a thing, but I kinda wonder what they meant by that bit. What if you're a Dalit and erupt? Do you get the gravy train or do you have to go to America? Do the other novas look down on you? Are you considered to be an honorary higher caste?<br /> <br /> The reality would probably be far more complicated depending on the actual individuals involved, but meh.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/173-morimaruvik","name":"Morimaruvik","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar173_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"126"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-14T01:38:33-07:00","id":513967,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Morimaruvik</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/trinity/trinity-continuum-aberrant/490262-define-india-syndrome?p=496805#post496805\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Caste is still a thing, but I kinda wonder what they meant by that bit. What if you're a Dalit and erupt? Do you get the gravy train or do you have to go to America? Do the other novas look down on you? Are you considered to be an honorary higher caste?<br /> <br /> The reality would probably be far more complicated depending on the actual individuals involved, but meh.</div> </div> </div> </div>That happened with Sin-Eater, of the Teragen, actually. She was one of the lower castes (&quot;Untouchable&quot;, if memory serves) before her eruption. Afterwards . . . well, there were a lot of wealthy/powerful Indian novas willing to hang out with the only known person with the ability to suppress Taint.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4624-one-vorlon","name":"One Vorlon","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4624_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"537"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-14T04:41:40-07:00","id":514030,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>One Vorlon</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/trinity/trinity-continuum-aberrant/490262-define-india-syndrome?p=513967#post513967\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">That happened with Sin-Eater, of the Teragen, actually. She was one of the lower castes (&quot;Untouchable&quot;, if memory serves) before her eruption. Afterwards . . . well, there were a lot of wealthy/powerful Indian novas willing to hang out with the only known person with the ability to suppress Taint.</div> </div> </div> </div>How on earth did I forget that? O.O","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/173-morimaruvik","name":"Morimaruvik","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar173_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"126"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-14T05:30:35-07:00","id":514053,"title":"","content":"Indian Castes are a fascinating subject that i know precious little about - but if the upper-most castes are supposed to be higher on the Hindu reincarnation ladder to their ultimate pinnacle, then there might be some relation to India Syndrome, if it were to be redefined. I think the system was designed in Vedic times to preserve genetic diversity, but it seems like a deeply involved topic that i might require some prerequisite reading to truly comprehend.<br /> Feel free to correct me if you know any better, tying india syndrome into the subcontinent's people, religion and traditions was always my preferred reading - because as far as i know Michael Archangel wasn't yoked with the syndrome despite meeting the definition presented on the previous page.<br /> Anyway, great point Morimaruvik and impressive memory work there, One Vorlon.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4458-nihilist","name":"Nihilist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4458_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"259"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}